      "ass:temporal-mixing"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Continuity and positivity of the fixed kernel on a smaller ball provide \\(k_0>0\\) and \\(\\eta\\in(0,1)\\) such that \\(K(v)\\ge k_0\\) when \\(\\|v\\|_2\\le\\eta\\). For \\(b\\le\\delta_0/\\eta\\), interiority and the density lower bound give\n\\[\np_b:=\\Pr\\{\\|X_{st}-z_{0s}\\|_2\\le\\eta b\\mid z_0\\}\\ge c_Xv_d\\eta^db^d=:c_pb^d,\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\nwhere \\(v_d\\) is the volume of the Euclidean unit ball. Let \\(M_b\\) count these visits. Since \\(r=0\\),\n\\[\nG_{sT}(z_{0s},b)\\ge\\frac{k_0M_b}{Tb^d}.\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nPartition the calendar into consecutive blocks of length \\(a_T\\), and separate odd from even blocks. By the definition of the beta coefficient, replacing successive blocks in either parity by independent copies changes the joint law in total variation by at most the sum of the intervening coefficients. The total replacement error is therefore\n\\[\n2K_T\\beta_0\\rho_0^{a_T}\\le CT^{-2}.\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\nFor an independent collection of block counts \\(Y_j\\in[0,a_T]\\), convexity gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E(e^{-\\theta Y_j})\\le1-\\frac{1-e^{-\\theta a_T}}{a_T}\\mathbb E(Y_j)\n\\le\\exp\\left\\{-\\frac{1-e^{-\\theta a_T}}{a_T}\\mathbb E(Y_j)\\right\\}.\n\\]\nExponential Markov's inequality with \\(\\theta=a_T^{-1}\\), applied to both parities and combined with (13), yields constants \\(c_1,C_1>0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{M_b\\le c_1Tb^d\\mid z_0\\}\\le C_1\\exp\\{-c_1Tb^d/a_T\\}+C_1T^{-2}.\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nThe corrected choice of \\(c_G\\) makes (12) coercive on the complement of the event in (14).\n\nLet\n\\[\nb_\\circ=(Tn_s)^{-1/(2\\lambda+d)}\\vee N^{-1/2}\\vee\\{a_T/T\\}^{1/d}\n\\]\nand let \\(b_q\\) be the first dyadic grid point not below \\(2^qb_\\circ\\). Equations (12)--(14) imply\n\\[\n\\Pr\\{h_s^\\star>Cb_q\\mid z_0\\}\\le C\\exp(-c2^{qd})+CT^{-2}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\nSumming the dyadic tail in (15) gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E[(h_s^\\star)^{2\\lambda}\\mid z_0]\\le Cb_\\circ^{2\\lambda}+CT^{-2}.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a_T\\asymp\\log(T\\vee2)\\) and \\(2\\lambda/d\\le2\\), this proves the asserted moment bound. The event \\(\\mathsf F_{\\mathrm{fb}}\\) is contained in the failure event at the top of the grid, giving its \\(CT^{-2}\\) bound. The exponent-\\(\\lambda\\) assertion follows by the same tail sum or by Cauchy--Schwarz. Under calendar independence no replacement is required and blocks of length one may be used, so \\(a_T\\) is replaced by \\(1\\).",
